Cursor is distributed under a **proprietary EULA** (not MIT like VS Code). Key terms:

| Section | Summary |
|---------|---------|
| **License Grant** | Limited, non-exclusive, non-transferable, freely revocable license |
| **Restrictions** | No copying, modifying, reverse engineering, decompiling, or creating derivative works |
| **Termination** | Cursor may terminate at any time for any reason |
| **Ownership** | All IP rights remain with Anysphere, Inc. |
| **Feedback** | Non-confidential; users grant perpetual, worldwide license to all feedback |
| **No Warranty** | "AS IS" and "AS AVAILABLE" basis; no warranties of any kind |
| **Liability Cap** | Maximum liability limited to $50.00 |
| **Arbitration** | **Mandatory individual arbitration** via JAMS (unless opted out within 30 days) |
| **Class Action Waiver** | No class actions, collective actions, or jury trials |
| **Governing Law** | California, USA |
| **Indemnification** | Users must defend and indemnify Cursor against claims arising from their use |
| **Export** | Subject to US export control laws |

**Opt-Out**: New users can reject the arbitration agreement within 30 days by emailing `legal@cursor.sh` with their full legal name.

**Contrast with VS Code**: VS Code is MIT-licensed (allowing modification, redistribution, derivative works). Cursor's EULA prohibits all of these — the code is closed-source and cannot be legally forked.

